{
  "id": "Compound::DB00983 - Disease::DOID:2841",
  "verbalization": "Formoterol, a member of the adrenergic beta2-agonists class, is implicated in the treatment of asthma through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Formoterol, through its inclusion in the broader category of adrenergic beta2-agonists, is linked to the drug Pirbuterol and Orciprenaline, both of which are known to treat asthma. This implies a shared mechanism of action, possibly involving bronchodilation and modulation of smooth muscle contraction in airway tissues. The presence of multiple overlapping pathways, including Indacaterol, further supports the notion that Formoterol may exert its therapeutic effects through a common biological mechanism, potentially involving the activation of beta2-adrenergic receptors. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of adrenergic agonists in asthma management, underscoring the importance of targeting these receptors for therapeutic intervention.",
